WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.766 --> 00:07.996 align:middle
Transcript of Chairman Warsh's
Press Conference July 29,

00:07.996 --> 00:11.486 align:middle
2026 CHAIRMAN WARSH.

00:11.486 --> 00:16.236 align:middle
Good day. My second
FOMC Committee meeting

00:16.316 --> 00:17.816 align:middle
as Chairman has come quickly.

00:18.886 --> 00:21.156 align:middle
It's probably too early
to call it a streak,

00:21.856 --> 00:24.696 align:middle
but our discussions again were
collegial and constructive.

00:25.246 --> 00:29.556 align:middle
I'm truly lucky to work
with colleagues so capable

00:29.556 --> 00:33.876 align:middle
and mission focused, and
so determined, like I am,

00:34.236 --> 00:36.336 align:middle
to sharpen the performance
of the Federal Reserve.

00:37.236 --> 00:39.656 align:middle
Today, as you know, our
Committee decided to vote

00:39.656 --> 00:43.296 align:middle
by a 9-to-3 vote to
maintain the target range

00:43.296 --> 00:46.976 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
at 3½ to 3  percent.

00:48.196 --> 00:51.406 align:middle
The Committee is continuing its
policy of making ample reserves

00:51.436 --> 00:52.356 align:middle
in the banking system.

00:53.766 --> 00:56.806 align:middle
The economy is showing
impressive resilience.

00:57.706 --> 01:00.976 align:middle
Even with recent shocks,
the trends are positive

01:01.166 --> 01:02.456 align:middle
and reveal solid growth.

01:03.526 --> 01:05.996 align:middle
Job gains have kept
pace with the workforce,

01:06.356 --> 01:08.476 align:middle
and the unemployment
rate has changed little.

01:10.136 --> 01:12.386 align:middle
Inflation remains
elevated relative

01:12.386 --> 01:13.936 align:middle
to the Committee's
2 percent goal.

01:14.996 --> 01:16.536 align:middle
The Committee remains resolute.

01:17.386 --> 01:20.996 align:middle
You've heard this before, but
we will deliver price stability.

01:22.486 --> 01:25.646 align:middle
As before, the policy statement
conveys just the facts.

01:26.276 --> 01:28.176 align:middle
It's steering clear
of forecasting,

01:29.006 --> 01:31.636 align:middle
a choice we consider
especially prudent

01:31.966 --> 01:33.486 align:middle
at these uncertain times.

01:34.586 --> 01:38.056 align:middle
Uncertainty, however, does
not mean a lack of clarity.

01:39.166 --> 01:42.216 align:middle
For some households, businesses,
and market professionals,

01:42.796 --> 01:46.866 align:middle
five years of high inflation
have left a mistaken impression

01:47.726 --> 01:51.756 align:middle
that's hard to shake-that the
Fed's implicit inflation target

01:51.846 --> 01:53.476 align:middle
was somehow above 2 percent.

01:54.336 --> 01:58.886 align:middle
Let me reiterate: There is
no soft inflation target;

01:59.656 --> 02:02.616 align:middle
there is no soft
implicit target-not

02:02.616 --> 02:04.116 align:middle
on this Committee's watch.

02:04.686 --> 02:07.196 align:middle
There's only a target,
and it's 2 percent.

02:08.686 --> 02:12.306 align:middle
Not one of my FOMC colleagues
is under any illusion.

02:13.486 --> 02:16.796 align:middle
We've begun a new
chapter, and we understand

02:16.796 --> 02:18.816 align:middle
that the five-plus
years of inflation

02:18.816 --> 02:25.366 align:middle
above target cannot be cured in
nine weeks-or by a single month

02:25.476 --> 02:27.626 align:middle
of modest price decreases.

02:28.356 --> 02:29.896 align:middle
This Fed will not waver.

02:29.896 --> 02:33.916 align:middle
Our credibility rests
on performing our duties

02:34.296 --> 02:36.766 align:middle
and delivering on
our responsibilities.

02:37.986 --> 02:39.486 align:middle
Americans are right to expect

02:39.546 --> 02:43.996 align:middle
that because our nation's
prosperity depends on it.

02:44.436 --> 02:46.136 align:middle
To the regulars here
in the press room,

02:46.616 --> 02:48.696 align:middle
today's assessment
might sound familiar.

02:49.716 --> 02:52.796 align:middle
Yet there was nothing
inertial about our discussions,

02:52.796 --> 02:55.116 align:middle
our policy, or our strategy.

02:56.256 --> 02:58.546 align:middle
Two economic developments
are worth highlighting.

02:59.426 --> 03:02.116 align:middle
The first is a very
notable change

03:02.116 --> 03:06.316 align:middle
since our last meeting
42 days ago: Nominal

03:06.316 --> 03:09.176 align:middle
and real yields are
materially higher

03:09.266 --> 03:10.736 align:middle
across the Treasury curve.

03:11.816 --> 03:15.066 align:middle
In fact, some of the increases
in market interest rates

03:15.486 --> 03:19.486 align:middle
between FOMC meetings are
among the most significant

03:19.706 --> 03:23.816 align:middle
in the last two decades, ranking
around the top decile or so.

03:24.346 --> 03:28.026 align:middle
But if the Committee didn't
change its policy rate,

03:28.196 --> 03:28.846 align:middle
what happened?

03:30.056 --> 03:33.176 align:middle
In the intermeeting period,
market attention centered

03:33.176 --> 03:36.916 align:middle
on real data and real
economic developments.

03:37.636 --> 03:41.096 align:middle
Prices reacted in real time
to incoming information,

03:42.476 --> 03:45.916 align:middle
and the reduction in forward
guidance may have been a factor.

03:47.346 --> 03:49.586 align:middle
Market participants are
learning to play the ball,

03:49.996 --> 03:54.706 align:middle
not the referee-and market
prices will continue to respond

03:55.276 --> 03:58.066 align:middle
in the direction and
magnitude they see fit.

03:59.286 --> 04:01.096 align:middle
This is, in my view, a change

04:01.096 --> 04:03.606 align:middle
for the better-and we're
just getting started.

04:04.836 --> 04:07.066 align:middle
After all, the central
bank need not always

04:07.066 --> 04:09.416 align:middle
and everywhere be the
center of attention.

04:10.896 --> 04:13.916 align:middle
I understand the desire
for rolling forecasts

04:13.916 --> 04:15.396 align:middle
and commentary from
this Committee.

04:16.686 --> 04:19.786 align:middle
But for our part, we need
to observe market reaction

04:19.786 --> 04:22.956 align:middle
to developments,
direct and unfiltered.

04:24.196 --> 04:27.496 align:middle
I want to stress, of
course, that decisions

04:27.496 --> 04:29.386 align:middle
by this Committee
matter a great deal.

04:30.846 --> 04:32.776 align:middle
And where necessary
and appropriate,

04:33.066 --> 04:35.896 align:middle
we will not hesitate to act.

04:36.596 --> 04:39.816 align:middle
A second economic
development is one that I noted

04:39.816 --> 04:42.446 align:middle
at the congressional
oversight hearings this month,

04:43.056 --> 04:44.386 align:middle
but it's worth repeating.

04:45.466 --> 04:48.996 align:middle
The most striking feature of
the economy is the strong growth

04:48.996 --> 04:49.926 align:middle
of business investment.

04:50.986 --> 04:53.746 align:middle
The surge in high-tech
capex has been remarkable.

04:54.796 --> 04:58.986 align:middle
But that does not necessarily
make the Fed's role any easier.

05:00.186 --> 05:03.186 align:middle
In the AI-related category
of high-tech equipment

05:03.186 --> 05:07.506 align:middle
and software, the most recent
data shows four-quarter growth

05:07.506 --> 05:09.906 align:middle
rates of nearly 20 percent.

05:11.336 --> 05:13.936 align:middle
This is helping to sustain
the healthy momentum

05:14.336 --> 05:15.596 align:middle
of manufacturing output.

05:16.526 --> 05:20.056 align:middle
More generally, capex
is preparing the ground

05:20.226 --> 05:21.136 align:middle
for future growth.

05:22.396 --> 05:26.396 align:middle
Nonetheless, the precise
timing and magnitude of effects

05:26.396 --> 05:31.086 align:middle
on the supply side
remain hard to predict.

05:31.086 --> 05:34.046 align:middle
FOMC meetings produce
policy decisions.

05:34.956 --> 05:38.266 align:middle
But just as important
is candid discussion

05:38.806 --> 05:40.906 align:middle
of the big things
that matter most.

05:42.346 --> 05:45.276 align:middle
That, too, is a priority in
this new chapter at the Fed.

05:46.356 --> 05:48.996 align:middle
In our meeting, vigorous
discussion centered

05:48.996 --> 05:51.626 align:middle
on four questions,
which I will enumerate.

05:52.626 --> 05:55.886 align:middle
First, we talked a lot
about the implications

05:56.276 --> 05:58.636 align:middle
of the past five years
of high inflation

05:59.096 --> 06:00.856 align:middle
on the current policy
conjuncture.

06:01.756 --> 06:06.606 align:middle
To, to echo an old phrase,
has the past really passed?

06:08.266 --> 06:11.016 align:middle
Second, my colleagues

06:11.016 --> 06:13.806 align:middle
and I considered the economic
shocks of recent years.

06:14.416 --> 06:17.026 align:middle
Strained supply chains
arising from the pandemic,

06:17.936 --> 06:22.086 align:middle
military conflicts,
energy supply disruptions,

06:22.866 --> 06:26.116 align:middle
substantial increases in
tariff rates, and, yes,

06:26.166 --> 06:28.546 align:middle
the surge in AI-related
investment.

06:29.976 --> 06:33.096 align:middle
These differ in their
sources-do they also differ

06:33.096 --> 06:35.446 align:middle
in their effects on
output and employment?

06:36.636 --> 06:39.546 align:middle
Third, we took up
the related question

06:39.546 --> 06:42.356 align:middle
of price increases
arising from shocks.

06:43.356 --> 06:46.396 align:middle
The business capex boom, for
example, is driving up prices

06:46.396 --> 06:50.846 align:middle
of memory and logic chips and
associated AI infrastructure.

06:52.106 --> 06:55.606 align:middle
Do those changes indicate a
broader inflationary dynamic,

06:56.456 --> 06:59.286 align:middle
or do we just focus on
them because they are

06:59.286 --> 07:00.566 align:middle
under the bright streetlight?

07:01.896 --> 07:06.156 align:middle
Finally, we discussed monetary
policy tools and strategies

07:06.776 --> 07:08.616 align:middle
for achieving stable prices.

07:09.896 --> 07:11.516 align:middle
If, as the Fed has long held,

07:11.966 --> 07:15.816 align:middle
interest rate policy should
be its primary monetary policy

07:15.816 --> 07:19.096 align:middle
instrument, how much
accommodation are we getting

07:19.096 --> 07:19.996 align:middle
from the balance sheet?

07:21.166 --> 07:24.536 align:middle
In all of this, our work
is advancing at the Fed.

07:25.796 --> 07:28.096 align:middle
We're asking the
right questions.

07:28.746 --> 07:33.506 align:middle
And in this consequential time,
we know how very much depends

07:33.596 --> 07:34.936 align:middle
on getting the right answers.

07:35.076 --> 07:38.266 align:middle
Of course, you've all arrived
with questions of your own,

07:38.486 --> 07:39.416 align:middle
so let's turn to them now.

07:39.756 --> 07:40.656 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:41.116 --> 07:41.936 align:middle
Let's start with Steve.

07:44.006 --> 07:45.726 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

07:45.726 --> 07:47.326 align:middle
Thank you for taking our
questions, Mr. Chairman.

07:47.356 --> 07:48.146 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

07:48.206 --> 07:48.836 align:middle
Sure. STEVE LIESMAN.

07:48.836 --> 07:50.796 align:middle
You've had a couple months now,

07:50.796 --> 07:55.186 align:middle
or nine weeks-whatever number
it is-to see the markets behave

07:55.706 --> 07:58.666 align:middle
in the absence of
forward guidance.

07:59.126 --> 08:03.286 align:middle
I'm wondering if you could tell
me-what message are you getting

08:03.396 --> 08:06.726 align:middle
from the markets as to where
policy ought to be right now?

08:06.726 --> 08:07.486 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

08:07.486 --> 08:10.036 align:middle
Yeah, so I think officially
it's eight weeks and four days.

08:10.466 --> 08:13.846 align:middle
But, I'm not counting.

08:13.846 --> 08:14.896 align:middle
[Laughter] The message

08:14.896 --> 08:17.056 align:middle
from markets is the
message from markets.

08:17.766 --> 08:19.696 align:middle
What I've really been
trying to do, Steve,

08:19.866 --> 08:22.686 align:middle
as I think you appreciate-and
your colleagues appreciate-is

08:22.686 --> 08:25.096 align:middle
getting an unfiltered
message from markets.

08:25.096 --> 08:26.386 align:middle
Getting a direct message.

08:26.896 --> 08:30.086 align:middle
Letting buyers and
sellers meet at prices

08:30.086 --> 08:32.926 align:middle
for Treasuries-for the foreign
exchange value of the dollar.

08:33.436 --> 08:35.906 align:middle
And then trying to judge
for ourselves-what does

08:35.906 --> 08:37.156 align:middle
that mean about our remit?

08:37.436 --> 08:39.006 align:middle
How are we doing on inflation?

08:39.006 --> 08:40.486 align:middle
How are we doing on employment?

08:41.766 --> 08:44.386 align:middle
We're trying not to interfere
with that market signal.

08:44.836 --> 08:47.426 align:middle
That's part of the reason
why we've been somewhat spare

08:47.426 --> 08:50.406 align:middle
on our words when we pulled
back from forward guidance.

08:50.856 --> 08:54.506 align:middle
So they're reacting to events,
I would say, much more directly

08:54.556 --> 08:56.776 align:middle
over the 42 days
since we last met.

08:57.376 --> 08:58.406 align:middle
This is a good thing.

08:58.946 --> 09:00.626 align:middle
As I mentioned in
the prepared remarks,

09:00.626 --> 09:02.206 align:middle
we've seen a material
tightening,

09:02.206 --> 09:05.236 align:middle
not just in nominal rates,
but in real rates, too,

09:05.876 --> 09:07.856 align:middle
and we're observing it.

09:08.246 --> 09:10.886 align:middle
We're trying to stay out
of that because, you know,

09:10.886 --> 09:12.566 align:middle
many of you might be interested

09:12.566 --> 09:15.286 align:middle
in our reaction function-we're
interested

09:15.286 --> 09:16.736 align:middle
in the reaction of
financial markets.

09:17.286 --> 09:17.856 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:17.966 --> 09:19.336 align:middle
I-I get that, Mr. Chairman.

09:19.336 --> 09:22.006 align:middle
And I guess the follow-up
question is,

09:22.756 --> 09:25.026 align:middle
if the markets are
talking to you,

09:25.126 --> 09:26.486 align:middle
what do you hear them saying?

09:26.946 --> 09:29.956 align:middle
And if it's "real rates are
higher," it would suggest

09:29.956 --> 09:32.696 align:middle
that that's where the
funds rate ought to go.

09:33.606 --> 09:33.926 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

09:33.926 --> 09:35.506 align:middle
Yeah, so- STEVE LIESMAN.

09:35.796 --> 09:36.446 align:middle
And-sorry.

09:36.446 --> 09:36.926 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

09:36.926 --> 09:38.636 align:middle
I'm sorry-it's your question.

09:38.636 --> 09:42.836 align:middle
So interpreting markets
is an imperfect business.

09:43.346 --> 09:45.876 align:middle
We central bankers,
like market pros,

09:46.296 --> 09:48.376 align:middle
can think these things
are overdetermined.

09:48.376 --> 09:51.116 align:middle
But let me offer
some speculation.

09:52.066 --> 09:56.106 align:middle
First, as we said in the
FOMC statement that you got

09:56.106 --> 10:00.556 align:middle
at two o'clock, the
economy-output is solid.

10:01.246 --> 10:07.776 align:middle
Capex and productivity are
strong-labor markets-solid,

10:07.996 --> 10:08.516 align:middle
steady.

10:08.866 --> 10:12.876 align:middle
The bond market-the
Treasury market-it seems

10:12.876 --> 10:14.066 align:middle
to be saying that as well.

10:14.656 --> 10:17.806 align:middle
If I were to try to break
down-just aggregate the Treasury

10:17.806 --> 10:20.246 align:middle
market signals-I wouldn't
be able to do it perfectly.

10:20.826 --> 10:23.756 align:middle
But the bond market's saying
many of those same things,

10:23.756 --> 10:26.366 align:middle
and that's why we're seeing
a tightening both in nominals

10:26.366 --> 10:29.246 align:middle
and in reals, even
while, at some level,

10:29.306 --> 10:31.986 align:middle
we haven't done much in 42 days.

10:32.246 --> 10:33.686 align:middle
The markets have
done quite a bit.

10:33.686 --> 10:34.406 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:37.776 --> 10:38.806 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

10:38.896 --> 10:39.116 align:middle
Thank you.

10:39.226 --> 10:40.916 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

10:41.596 --> 10:43.506 align:middle
You seem to have got the
family fight you were

10:43.506 --> 10:45.306 align:middle
after at this meeting.

10:45.646 --> 10:48.006 align:middle
[Laughter] We saw three-we
saw three dissents.

10:48.936 --> 10:50.886 align:middle
Could you characterize
the arguments

10:50.886 --> 10:52.726 align:middle
that those dissenters
put forward, please,

10:52.726 --> 10:55.846 align:middle
and tell us a little bit
why you weren't persuaded

10:55.896 --> 10:56.976 align:middle
by them at this stage?

10:57.146 --> 10:57.446 align:middle
Thank you.

10:57.446 --> 10:57.836 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

10:57.836 --> 10:59.956 align:middle
So I guess I shouldn't give
you their best arguments.

10:59.956 --> 11:01.416 align:middle
I'll give you some others.

11:02.196 --> 11:03.306 align:middle
[Laughter] So you're right.

11:03.306 --> 11:05.696 align:middle
I asked for a good family
fight, and I got one.

11:06.376 --> 11:08.906 align:middle
That's the purpose;
that's the design feature.

11:09.696 --> 11:12.936 align:middle
I come into this meeting,
even this press conference,

11:13.136 --> 11:15.796 align:middle
heartened by what I've
experienced the last two days.

11:16.386 --> 11:19.476 align:middle
Most of our discussion were on
the big questions that matter

11:19.476 --> 11:22.096 align:middle
to the conduct of
monetary policy.

11:22.796 --> 11:26.276 align:middle
We, we didn't sort of hide from
them-we weren't scared of them.

11:26.456 --> 11:30.136 align:middle
There was a lot more interaction
between and among my colleagues.

11:30.566 --> 11:32.546 align:middle
It was a real family fight.

11:32.546 --> 11:35.066 align:middle
My view, which you've
long heard,

11:35.566 --> 11:38.226 align:middle
is that's the better
way to get policy right.

11:38.336 --> 11:39.406 align:middle
That's our North Star.

11:39.946 --> 11:42.656 align:middle
So there was a lot of agreement

11:42.656 --> 11:45.796 align:middle
that I heard-that
we have the powers,

11:45.796 --> 11:49.996 align:middle
the tools-also the authority
to deliver stable prices.

11:49.996 --> 11:53.706 align:middle
No walking back from our
responsibilities-there was a

11:53.706 --> 11:56.676 align:middle
large majority support for
the decision that we made

11:56.676 --> 11:59.396 align:middle
in the room, but I also
want to leave you, Claire,

11:59.396 --> 12:00.446 align:middle
with one other impression.

12:00.446 --> 12:02.566 align:middle
There was nothing inertial
about that discussion.

12:03.356 --> 12:06.466 align:middle
It was an active, robust
discussion about what's

12:06.466 --> 12:09.916 align:middle
in the full range of what
we can do, and might want

12:09.916 --> 12:11.456 align:middle
to do, in the period ahead.

12:12.796 --> 12:14.736 align:middle
You characterized
accurately-there was a

12:14.736 --> 12:17.486 align:middle
disagreement about
a decision today.

12:17.906 --> 12:19.266 align:middle
I would say that doesn't sort

12:19.266 --> 12:22.016 align:middle
of capture the full
essence of the discussion.

12:22.796 --> 12:26.786 align:middle
The path to central bank
heaven requires delivering

12:26.786 --> 12:27.436 align:middle
on our remit.

12:27.576 --> 12:30.486 align:middle
These days, that means
delivering on price stability.

12:31.376 --> 12:32.756 align:middle
I wouldn't measure that path

12:32.756 --> 12:35.496 align:middle
in 42 days-or any one
particular meeting.

12:35.966 --> 12:38.386 align:middle
And I came out of that
meeting even more confident

12:38.466 --> 12:41.006 align:middle
that this is the right
team to win the battle

12:41.006 --> 12:42.396 align:middle
against high inflation.

12:42.716 --> 12:42.986 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

12:43.036 --> 12:45.456 align:middle
How much do you think
not going in July was

12:45.496 --> 12:47.936 align:middle
down to the cool CPI
"print" for June?

12:47.936 --> 12:49.156 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

12:49.156 --> 12:55.176 align:middle
So, in two words, not much-not
much-I'd like to believe

12:55.176 --> 12:58.306 align:middle
that the Committee
shares my views,

12:58.306 --> 13:01.256 align:middle
which is the historic problem

13:01.256 --> 13:04.546 align:middle
with data dependence is the
data and the dependence.

13:05.166 --> 13:09.256 align:middle
We are not relying on any
one individual piece of data

13:09.546 --> 13:12.956 align:middle
as cover or as an
excuse or as validation.

13:13.806 --> 13:17.126 align:middle
What I care about, and what I
think the Committee cares about,

13:17.126 --> 13:20.366 align:middle
is trends on the data.

13:20.366 --> 13:23.016 align:middle
Sure, we got some
encouraging inflation data.

13:23.016 --> 13:26.606 align:middle
I think at the meeting 42
days ago I said something like

13:26.606 --> 13:29.776 align:middle
"63 months of inflation
above target."

13:29.776 --> 13:33.186 align:middle
I didn't say "64," though
the final calculation might,

13:33.186 --> 13:34.376 align:middle
might be a close one.

13:34.906 --> 13:37.246 align:middle
So we'll be-we'll be
watching inflation data

13:37.246 --> 13:40.076 align:middle
over the period ahead,
but I also don't want you

13:40.076 --> 13:42.276 align:middle
to leave the misimpression
that we're sort

13:42.276 --> 13:43.736 align:middle
of breathlessly waiting that.

13:44.316 --> 13:49.446 align:middle
I've called for a task force
to revisit both the private

13:49.446 --> 13:52.426 align:middle
and public data we use to
make our decisionmaking.

13:52.686 --> 13:54.546 align:middle
That task force is
out doing their work.

13:54.546 --> 13:56.566 align:middle
I'll be checking back in
with them in the next couple

13:56.566 --> 13:59.936 align:middle
of weeks, but I wouldn't
say we overly relied on,

13:59.936 --> 14:02.236 align:middle
on any one piece of data,
including that data,

14:02.236 --> 14:04.086 align:middle
which surprised some
a couple of weeks ago.

14:05.346 --> 14:05.736 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:06.026 --> 14:08.186 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

14:08.646 --> 14:10.046 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Warsh, thanks.

14:10.126 --> 14:11.076 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

14:11.076 --> 14:12.846 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

14:13.076 --> 14:14.326 align:middle
So the fed funds rate is now

14:14.326 --> 14:17.666 align:middle
about 75 basis points below the
two-year yield-suggests markets

14:17.666 --> 14:19.926 align:middle
think you'll have to tighten
eventually-about 100 basis

14:19.926 --> 14:21.876 align:middle
points below most
Taylor rule estimates.

14:22.286 --> 14:24.406 align:middle
You're hitting your employment
mandate; inflation stays high.

14:24.676 --> 14:26.126 align:middle
Why should rates
not be higher today?

14:26.126 --> 14:26.796 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

14:27.346 --> 14:29.036 align:middle
There's a lot in there, Neil.

14:29.976 --> 14:35.466 align:middle
So rates are higher today
than they were 42 days ago.

14:35.466 --> 14:39.656 align:middle
Markets have made decisions
because we stepped back,

14:39.656 --> 14:42.216 align:middle
in part, from trying
to influence those.

14:42.776 --> 14:47.606 align:middle
Market judgments have moved
up on what nominal rates are

14:47.966 --> 14:49.826 align:middle
across the Treasury curve.

14:50.636 --> 14:54.436 align:middle
That doesn't mean we take
them as-by dictation,

14:54.436 --> 14:55.816 align:middle
but we're observing them.

14:56.506 --> 14:59.176 align:middle
So I think it's a
mischaracterization to say

14:59.176 --> 15:02.576 align:middle
that markets haven't reacted
because we didn't move today.

15:02.576 --> 15:05.286 align:middle
Markets are reacting
in real time.

15:05.286 --> 15:08.626 align:middle
In the period ahead, we've got
important decisions to make

15:08.626 --> 15:09.986 align:middle
about the policy rate.

15:10.486 --> 15:12.636 align:middle
Markets in the intervening
period, I think,

15:12.636 --> 15:15.166 align:middle
have quite a bit of
decisions to make.

15:15.656 --> 15:17.036 align:middle
I'll see if I can
put it this way:

15:17.736 --> 15:22.066 align:middle
Monetary policy matters
not just by what we say,

15:23.036 --> 15:26.306 align:middle
or even what we do-monetary
policy matters

15:26.356 --> 15:28.166 align:middle
by how it affects
the real economy.

15:28.876 --> 15:32.296 align:middle
And these prices that we see
in financial markets is one

15:32.296 --> 15:35.696 align:middle
of the many ways in which
it affects the real economy.

15:36.146 --> 15:37.916 align:middle
We'll be continuing to watch

15:37.916 --> 15:41.006 align:middle
that market information-see
how it responds

15:41.006 --> 15:44.636 align:middle
to incoming events-and that can
help inform our decisionmaking

15:44.636 --> 15:45.976 align:middle
when we meet in seven
or eight weeks.

15:45.976 --> 15:46.196 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

15:46.196 --> 15:47.246 align:middle
How would you characterize,

15:47.246 --> 15:50.056 align:middle
of the-in the family fight
the last couple days-of you

15:50.056 --> 15:51.776 align:middle
and the other eight
members who wanted to hold?

15:52.016 --> 15:53.036 align:middle
Was that a strong conviction,

15:53.036 --> 15:55.426 align:middle
or was that a hair-trigger
close call

15:55.426 --> 15:56.486 align:middle
on holding versus tightening?

15:57.546 --> 15:57.896 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

15:57.896 --> 16:01.016 align:middle
Well, I think, you know, the
vote-the vote was 9 to 3.

16:01.096 --> 16:06.566 align:middle
The broader discussion,
to my ear, over the course

16:06.566 --> 16:09.526 align:middle
of the last days showed
a lot of agreement

16:09.526 --> 16:13.516 align:middle
on the hard questions-the four
questions I raised at the outset

16:14.166 --> 16:18.006 align:middle
about what's really happening
in the economy with the shocks

16:18.006 --> 16:19.236 align:middle
and absent the shocks.

16:19.826 --> 16:22.546 align:middle
What are our tools
and our capabilities?

16:22.646 --> 16:27.506 align:middle
What's the effect on
prices and on output?

16:28.046 --> 16:30.756 align:middle
I heard a lot of
commonality on the questions.

16:31.066 --> 16:32.796 align:middle
Were there different
"leans" on the answers?

16:32.796 --> 16:33.806 align:middle
You bet there was.

16:34.186 --> 16:36.716 align:middle
So could people come to
different conclusions?

16:36.716 --> 16:37.576 align:middle
Absolutely.

16:38.076 --> 16:42.016 align:middle
But my own judgment
is this is a period

16:42.016 --> 16:45.446 align:middle
of watchful thinking,
not watchful waiting.

16:45.926 --> 16:49.046 align:middle
And I think the score on
that vote was unanimous.

16:49.666 --> 16:50.306 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:50.906 --> 16:52.106 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

16:55.706 --> 16:57.326 align:middle
Thank you, Colby Smith
with the New York Times.

16:57.776 --> 16:59.906 align:middle
You've mentioned that looking
at the Fed's policy tools

16:59.906 --> 17:02.316 align:middle
as one element of a
three-pronged strategy

17:02.316 --> 17:04.116 align:middle
to address the inflation
problem.

17:04.636 --> 17:08.026 align:middle
So I'm curious how you view the
effectiveness of those tools.

17:08.456 --> 17:12.416 align:middle
If inflation is too high and not
coming down, is the best remedy

17:12.416 --> 17:13.306 align:middle
to raise interest rates?

17:14.136 --> 17:14.816 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

17:14.856 --> 17:18.266 align:middle
So that's-that was the
discussion the last two days.

17:18.606 --> 17:21.136 align:middle
Is that the dominant remedy?

17:21.916 --> 17:24.796 align:middle
If inflation continues
to be elevated

17:24.796 --> 17:26.196 align:middle
through the forecast period,

17:26.736 --> 17:29.166 align:middle
interest rates could well
be part of that solution.

17:29.706 --> 17:31.496 align:middle
But I wouldn't say
it's in isolation.

17:31.886 --> 17:35.926 align:middle
I tried to describe in my
remarks today the point

17:35.926 --> 17:38.046 align:middle
that I made in-to the
oversight committees a couple

17:38.046 --> 17:38.626 align:middle
of weeks ago.

17:38.726 --> 17:42.216 align:middle
I think there was a
misimpression-by some

17:42.216 --> 17:44.206 align:middle
in financial markets,
by some households

17:44.206 --> 17:46.566 align:middle
and businesses-that
central bankers

17:46.566 --> 17:48.916 align:middle
like me-we set a 2
percent inflation target,

17:49.536 --> 17:51.706 align:middle
but maybe we were more tolerable

17:51.706 --> 17:53.666 align:middle
of a somewhat higher
inflation target.

17:53.666 --> 17:56.656 align:middle
In economics, we'd call that
the revealed preference.

17:57.096 --> 17:59.846 align:middle
And so might it have been
rational for people to think,

17:59.846 --> 18:02.336 align:middle
"Well, their inflation
target's somewhat higher"?

18:03.146 --> 18:06.056 align:middle
The-what I heard in the last two
days-what I've heard in eight

18:06.056 --> 18:07.506 align:middle
and a half weeks is "no."

18:08.046 --> 18:10.646 align:middle
We will deliver the 2
percent inflation target.

18:10.736 --> 18:13.966 align:middle
That is the Committee's
definition of price stability.

18:14.636 --> 18:18.506 align:middle
So one way, absent the
tools that you reference,

18:18.646 --> 18:21.056 align:middle
to ensure that we
get there is ensure

18:21.056 --> 18:23.826 align:middle
that expectations are centered
around the right number.

18:24.316 --> 18:26.946 align:middle
And I think we've made
some progress on that.

18:27.116 --> 18:29.586 align:middle
I am not suggesting
we're done on that.

18:29.906 --> 18:31.336 align:middle
It's worth reiterating.

18:31.776 --> 18:35.796 align:middle
And, ultimately, the business
we're in, Colby, is performance.

18:35.896 --> 18:38.266 align:middle
We're going to be
judged by how we perform.

18:38.826 --> 18:40.896 align:middle
And that's what we intend to do

18:40.896 --> 18:44.126 align:middle
with the inflation target-making
clear expectations is one part

18:44.126 --> 18:44.336 align:middle
of it.

18:44.876 --> 18:48.136 align:middle
Making sure we demonstrate
we're responsible

18:48.136 --> 18:51.386 align:middle
for it-we're not blaming is
another-and our policy tools,

18:51.386 --> 18:52.836 align:middle
like you referenced,
is the third

18:52.836 --> 18:54.856 align:middle
and equally consequential part.

18:54.856 --> 18:55.706 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

18:55.706 --> 18:58.306 align:middle
How are you factoring the
fact that a large portion

18:58.386 --> 19:00.586 align:middle
of the inflation
overshoot is being caused

19:00.626 --> 19:02.906 align:middle
by supply shocks-as
it's mentioned again in,

19:02.906 --> 19:05.986 align:middle
in the statement-does that
blunt the effectiveness

19:05.986 --> 19:07.026 align:middle
of rate hikes in your view?

19:07.776 --> 19:08.566 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

19:08.626 --> 19:11.306 align:middle
First, on the premise of
your question, it was almost

19:11.306 --> 19:12.856 align:middle
as if you were listening
to our discussion

19:12.856 --> 19:14.236 align:middle
in the last day and a half.

19:14.946 --> 19:18.016 align:middle
A lot of our focus was
on trying to understand

19:18.306 --> 19:22.916 align:middle
and identify underlying
inflation dynamics amid shocks.

19:24.206 --> 19:25.836 align:middle
We take these shocks seriously.

19:26.046 --> 19:27.736 align:middle
There've been a series of them

19:27.736 --> 19:30.016 align:middle
that have been hitting
this economy.

19:30.506 --> 19:31.826 align:middle
We're not looking
through them and saying,

19:31.826 --> 19:32.856 align:middle
"Oh, they don't matter."

19:32.856 --> 19:36.766 align:middle
But we're trying to understand
is-to what extent are these

19:36.766 --> 19:39.666 align:middle
shocks broadening in
their effect-broadening

19:39.666 --> 19:43.046 align:middle
in their impact on prices that
are quite far removed from it?

19:43.046 --> 19:48.136 align:middle
Our goal is to have growth that
is broadening and inflation

19:48.136 --> 19:51.006 align:middle
that is becoming more
limited-more circumscribed.

19:51.556 --> 19:54.236 align:middle
I'll be the first to admit
the shocks make this job

19:54.236 --> 19:56.496 align:middle
and this policy conjuncture
a little tougher.

19:57.086 --> 20:00.816 align:middle
But that's among the chief
questions we've asked ourselves.

20:01.256 --> 20:03.496 align:middle
And, around the room, people
have different views on it.

20:03.496 --> 20:06.946 align:middle
I tend to think, in the coming
months, we're going to refine

20:06.946 --> 20:09.976 align:middle
that view and have a better
judgment, and we're going

20:09.976 --> 20:12.336 align:middle
to have market prices trying
to help inform it, too.

20:12.336 --> 20:13.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:16.806 --> 20:18.776 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

20:18.776 --> 20:19.586 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

20:19.586 --> 20:21.176 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
FOX Business.

20:21.626 --> 20:22.846 align:middle
I guess I want to drill

20:22.846 --> 20:24.636 align:middle
down maybe a little
bit-what specifically

20:24.866 --> 20:26.466 align:middle
in your mind would
be the argument,

20:26.466 --> 20:27.656 align:middle
then, for a pause today?

20:28.466 --> 20:28.986 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

20:28.986 --> 20:32.536 align:middle
So I wouldn't characterize what
we did as anything like a pause.

20:32.786 --> 20:37.366 align:middle
I would characterize what
we did as a rigorous review

20:37.366 --> 20:39.266 align:middle
of the economic situation.

20:40.106 --> 20:43.706 align:middle
I would characterize
what we did as a review

20:43.706 --> 20:45.926 align:middle
of the big, hard questions.

20:46.476 --> 20:50.796 align:middle
And I'd characterize it as a
view of what our own homework is

20:51.066 --> 20:54.386 align:middle
to try to resolve those
questions in the period ahead.

20:56.036 --> 20:59.146 align:middle
If you were to try to
force a description

20:59.146 --> 21:03.026 align:middle
that this was a pause, I would
say financial market prices

21:03.026 --> 21:04.406 align:middle
would take the other
side of that.

21:04.716 --> 21:08.216 align:middle
Financial market prices in
this intermeeting period-they

21:08.216 --> 21:08.706 align:middle
didn't pause.

21:08.706 --> 21:11.046 align:middle
They reacted to the
inflation data

21:11.046 --> 21:14.876 align:middle
in one direction-strong economic
growth in the other direction.

21:15.046 --> 21:18.376 align:middle
And nominal and real
rates went up.

21:19.356 --> 21:23.946 align:middle
Did the Fed take an explicit
change in its policy rate today?

21:24.176 --> 21:26.956 align:middle
No. But I think that's the
beginning of the story,

21:26.956 --> 21:27.806 align:middle
not the end of the story.

21:27.806 --> 21:28.186 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

21:28.186 --> 21:30.336 align:middle
And, and, if I could, I did want
to ask-not forward guidance,

21:30.336 --> 21:32.216 align:middle
but looking forward
[laughter]-traditionally,

21:32.216 --> 21:35.426 align:middle
a Federal Reserve Chairman uses
the Jackson Hole summit as,

21:35.426 --> 21:37.676 align:middle
as sort of a reset
of monetary policy.

21:37.676 --> 21:39.016 align:middle
How do you look at the speech

21:39.256 --> 21:40.376 align:middle
that you're going
to make in August?

21:40.916 --> 21:41.366 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

21:41.366 --> 21:43.336 align:middle
I look at it like a blank
piece of paper right now.

21:43.466 --> 21:47.476 align:middle
I, I have not begun
consideration

21:47.476 --> 21:50.566 align:middle
with the incredible
team here what would go

21:50.566 --> 21:51.626 align:middle
into that document.

21:52.096 --> 21:54.836 align:middle
I think you characterized
it correctly.

21:54.836 --> 21:58.316 align:middle
Historically, at least for my
first tour of duty at the Fed,

21:58.316 --> 22:00.326 align:middle
the more recent periods-it
would be sort

22:00.326 --> 22:03.736 align:middle
of a setting-up speech more
often than not-of what was going

22:03.736 --> 22:04.986 align:middle
to be happening in the fall.

22:05.056 --> 22:07.206 align:middle
I haven't made any
judgments on that.

22:07.756 --> 22:10.496 align:middle
But those are judgments
we'll have to come to.

22:11.016 --> 22:15.126 align:middle
If I could, in the high mountain
air in Jackson, Wyoming,

22:15.576 --> 22:18.756 align:middle
I'd like to also frame
the big questions.

22:19.206 --> 22:22.716 align:middle
There is a tendency, especially
with the proliferation

22:22.716 --> 22:25.486 align:middle
of meetings and press
conferences, to get caught

22:25.486 --> 22:29.446 align:middle
up in the myopic: "Did you do
this by a quarter, or do that?"

22:30.366 --> 22:32.496 align:middle
Ultimately, whether we deliver

22:32.496 --> 22:35.526 align:middle
on price stability matters
some-the decisions we make

22:35.526 --> 22:37.486 align:middle
in six- or seven- or
eight-week periods.

22:37.786 --> 22:39.946 align:middle
But they matter more-what
are the big questions?

22:40.036 --> 22:41.776 align:middle
What's really happening
with productivity?

22:42.266 --> 22:44.696 align:middle
What's really happening
with demographics?

22:45.336 --> 22:49.276 align:middle
What's really happening to the
global economy amid the shocks?

22:49.696 --> 22:51.936 align:middle
Haven't made a decision
whether it's going

22:51.936 --> 22:54.006 align:middle
to be a big-picture speech
or whether it's going

22:54.006 --> 22:57.496 align:middle
to be a more traditional set up
for all the action we're going

22:57.496 --> 22:58.756 align:middle
to have between September

22:58.756 --> 23:01.986 align:middle
and December-I will tell you
one other thing that I am doing

23:01.986 --> 23:04.596 align:middle
between now and Jackson
Hole-is I'm checking

23:04.596 --> 23:05.706 align:middle
with those task forces.

23:06.256 --> 23:07.846 align:middle
My first principle

23:07.846 --> 23:11.486 align:middle
of establishing a task force
is-find the best subject matter

23:11.486 --> 23:12.906 align:middle
experts anywhere in the world

23:13.546 --> 23:16.256 align:middle
and put them together-especially
put them together

23:16.256 --> 23:17.926 align:middle
with other people who
might disagree with them.

23:17.926 --> 23:19.376 align:middle
In the next couple weeks,

23:19.376 --> 23:23.506 align:middle
I'm going to be doing a check
back in-giving them time to sort

23:23.506 --> 23:26.336 align:middle
of think hard about their
agenda, their debate,

23:26.836 --> 23:29.546 align:middle
their schedule, and when they
might be ready for prime time.

23:29.926 --> 23:32.916 align:middle
I'll be doing a little bit of
that checking, and that may

23:32.916 --> 23:35.256 align:middle
or may not inform anything
I have to say in Jackson.

23:36.726 --> 23:37.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:38.916 --> 23:40.436 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

23:41.416 --> 23:42.756 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

23:43.546 --> 23:46.466 align:middle
Chairman Warsh, I want to
follow up on Colby's question

23:46.656 --> 23:48.986 align:middle
about policy transmission.

23:49.366 --> 23:50.906 align:middle
You've said there's
no cruel choice

23:50.946 --> 23:53.266 align:middle
between stable prices
and full employment.

23:54.516 --> 23:56.856 align:middle
Rates bring inflation
down by cooling demand.

23:57.326 --> 23:59.506 align:middle
It's generally thought that can
show up in the labor market.

23:59.996 --> 24:03.626 align:middle
If that's not the channel
you're relying on, what is?

24:03.906 --> 24:04.356 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

24:04.356 --> 24:07.596 align:middle
Yeah, so let me go back
to first principles, Nick.

24:07.596 --> 24:14.946 align:middle
I don't believe that either
part of our mandate is generally

24:14.946 --> 24:16.216 align:middle
at war with the other part.

24:16.216 --> 24:19.436 align:middle
I do not believe
that price stability

24:19.436 --> 24:23.346 align:middle
and full employment is
an either-or proposition.

24:23.346 --> 24:25.136 align:middle
There have been policymakers

24:25.256 --> 24:28.056 align:middle
over the last several
generations who have thought

24:28.056 --> 24:29.856 align:middle
that there is a strict
tradeoff there.

24:30.576 --> 24:31.766 align:middle
That isn't my judgment.

24:31.996 --> 24:38.066 align:middle
In fact, my judgment is, if and
when we deliver on our remit,

24:38.366 --> 24:40.866 align:middle
we're going to be
satisfying both prongs.

24:41.326 --> 24:43.906 align:middle
We're going to have price
stability and full employment.

24:44.006 --> 24:46.886 align:middle
And, in fact, if you
want to do the most harm

24:46.886 --> 24:50.416 align:middle
to the labor markets,
you would run a period

24:50.416 --> 24:52.296 align:middle
of high inflation
that's variable

24:52.296 --> 24:55.756 align:middle
such that employers-businesses
-wouldn't really know what's

24:55.756 --> 24:56.256 align:middle
going on.

24:56.846 --> 25:00.576 align:middle
So I think the two parts of our
mandate are equally important.

25:00.576 --> 25:02.376 align:middle
We have no legislative
orphans here.

25:03.026 --> 25:05.386 align:middle
I've been talking mostly
about price stability

25:05.386 --> 25:07.686 align:middle
because we're doing
pretty well, collectively,

25:07.686 --> 25:10.886 align:middle
as a country-as policymakers-on
the full-employment side.

25:11.556 --> 25:14.166 align:middle
But we're doing considerably
less well on prices.

25:14.166 --> 25:15.916 align:middle
That's why we describe
them as elevated,

25:15.916 --> 25:18.216 align:middle
and that's what's taken
most of our discussion.

25:18.996 --> 25:23.396 align:middle
In terms of transmission
mechanisms of monetary policy,

25:23.496 --> 25:25.846 align:middle
I think different tools work

25:25.976 --> 25:28.176 align:middle
through different
transmission mechanisms.

25:28.176 --> 25:30.606 align:middle
The interest rates work
through lending channels

25:30.606 --> 25:33.056 align:middle
and credit channels-maybe
confidence channels

25:33.056 --> 25:34.086 align:middle
and foreign exchange.

25:34.606 --> 25:36.806 align:middle
The balance sheet probably works
through some other channels,

25:36.806 --> 25:38.996 align:middle
like signaling and
portfolio balance.

25:39.606 --> 25:40.846 align:middle
We're keeping full abreast

25:40.846 --> 25:43.066 align:middle
of all these tools
and making policy.

25:43.586 --> 25:46.476 align:middle
But if the suggestion
is somehow we're going

25:46.476 --> 25:50.146 align:middle
to be fine-tuning aggregate
demand so it catches supply,

25:50.906 --> 25:52.526 align:middle
that's not my mental model.

25:52.556 --> 25:55.086 align:middle
I don't think we're great
in the fine-tuning business.

25:55.156 --> 25:58.896 align:middle
We're trying to get supply and
demand in broad order, but,

25:58.896 --> 26:00.986 align:middle
really, what we're doing
as we sit here today

26:00.986 --> 26:04.166 align:middle
at this press conference-is I
think we've got a reasonable

26:04.166 --> 26:08.006 align:middle
sense of what aggregate demand
looks like in this economy.

26:08.616 --> 26:10.996 align:middle
We're inferring aggregate
supply.

26:11.206 --> 26:14.086 align:middle
We're making a judgment
about what productivity is.

26:14.656 --> 26:17.786 align:middle
And, in some sense, there's a
race between supply and demand.

26:18.456 --> 26:22.346 align:middle
And the surge in business capex
in and around AI is making

26:22.796 --> 26:25.846 align:middle
that calculation a
little harder to judge,

26:25.906 --> 26:26.876 align:middle
but in the period ahead,

26:26.876 --> 26:28.296 align:middle
we're going to be trying
to judge just that.

26:28.296 --> 26:28.756 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

26:28.756 --> 26:29.476 align:middle
And if I could ask,

26:29.686 --> 26:31.856 align:middle
where exactly was the
disagreement today?

26:31.856 --> 26:33.956 align:middle
Was it about the
inflation forecast,

26:34.426 --> 26:38.986 align:middle
or was it something more around
the, the risks-the, the tactics?

26:40.066 --> 26:40.376 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

26:40.376 --> 26:40.866 align:middle
Yeah, so I'm going

26:40.866 --> 26:44.036 align:middle
to let the-I'll let the
dissenters speak for themselves.

26:44.466 --> 26:49.176 align:middle
The way I heard it over the
last two days was overwhelming

26:49.176 --> 26:53.586 align:middle
agreement on objectives and
authority and commitment.

26:54.146 --> 26:57.726 align:middle
I didn't hear anybody
walking away from it.

26:57.726 --> 26:59.566 align:middle
The judgments as to how best

26:59.566 --> 27:02.716 align:middle
to achieve the price
stability-that was probably the

27:02.716 --> 27:04.326 align:middle
question that we were
trying to answer.

27:04.916 --> 27:07.286 align:middle
What's the best move?

27:07.436 --> 27:09.316 align:middle
What's the best strategy?

27:09.706 --> 27:11.756 align:middle
What's the best way
to achieve it?

27:12.306 --> 27:15.476 align:middle
And a second question that
was asked is, when do we need

27:15.476 --> 27:17.526 align:middle
to make those harder calls?

27:17.526 --> 27:19.536 align:middle
When do we need to
make those decisions?

27:20.136 --> 27:24.466 align:middle
And like I said to one of your
colleagues, I was comforted

27:24.776 --> 27:28.776 align:middle
that markets in the intermeeting
period weren't reacting

27:28.776 --> 27:30.636 align:middle
to us-they weren't
reacting to dots

27:30.636 --> 27:33.056 align:middle
or to speeches-they
appeared more than ever

27:33.486 --> 27:35.816 align:middle
to be reacting to
real-time events.

27:36.226 --> 27:41.766 align:middle
So they're gauging themselves
the-how restrictive the Treasury

27:41.766 --> 27:42.776 align:middle
curve should be.

27:43.316 --> 27:46.416 align:middle
And that, I think, has
been a useful development.

27:46.666 --> 27:49.226 align:middle
We don't endorse any
particular market move,

27:49.736 --> 27:52.886 align:middle
but it also suggests we
observe them with keen interest.

27:54.186 --> 27:55.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:57.246 --> 27:58.856 align:middle
Jonnelle. JONNELLE MARTE.

27:58.856 --> 28:00.416 align:middle
Jonnelle Marte with Bloomberg.

28:00.696 --> 28:05.036 align:middle
Following up on that, there was
more uncertainty in the markets

28:05.036 --> 28:06.776 align:middle
about what the Fed would
do at this meeting.

28:07.536 --> 28:09.746 align:middle
To some extent you might think
that's what you want to see,

28:09.886 --> 28:12.056 align:middle
but my question is
about-is there a point

28:12.056 --> 28:14.186 align:middle
at which you would not want
to surprise the markets

28:14.186 --> 28:17.066 align:middle
if they were pricing in
something with higher certainty

28:17.066 --> 28:19.766 align:middle
that was opposite of what
you were intending to do?

28:20.136 --> 28:22.276 align:middle
What are the risks that you
see associated with that?

28:22.276 --> 28:22.736 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

28:22.736 --> 28:24.556 align:middle
Yeah, so it's a good question.

28:24.766 --> 28:26.916 align:middle
Surprise is not the
objective function.

28:27.766 --> 28:30.676 align:middle
Surprise is not what
we're solving for.

28:31.686 --> 28:33.246 align:middle
We have a clear North Star.

28:33.246 --> 28:36.036 align:middle
What we're solving for is how
to make the best decisions.

28:36.926 --> 28:40.246 align:middle
Almost everything else should
be in service to that goal.

28:41.266 --> 28:46.226 align:middle
By not spoon-feeding markets,
by not previewing our decisions,

28:46.696 --> 28:50.646 align:middle
by not sort of giving nudges
and leans, my colleagues

28:50.646 --> 28:51.356 align:middle
and I have found

28:51.356 --> 28:54.696 align:middle
in the intermeeting period
what we're getting is the views

28:54.696 --> 28:57.106 align:middle
from a very accomplished
[group of] economists.

28:57.106 --> 28:59.416 align:middle
That's the internals
of financial markets.

29:00.186 --> 29:04.266 align:middle
Instead of just repeating or
echoing what we're saying back

29:04.346 --> 29:07.626 align:middle
to us, they're giving us
somewhat-not perfect-their

29:07.626 --> 29:09.556 align:middle
own judgment.

29:10.126 --> 29:13.606 align:middle
So surprises are
not the objective.

29:14.196 --> 29:17.436 align:middle
But, at the same time, I
would say, we didn't come

29:17.436 --> 29:21.346 align:middle
into this meeting feeling
constrained by the full range

29:21.346 --> 29:22.776 align:middle
of alternatives we
had in front of us.

29:22.776 --> 29:23.206 align:middle
JONNELLE MARTE.

29:23.296 --> 29:25.326 align:middle
So some of your peers
have continued

29:25.326 --> 29:28.226 align:middle
to discuss how they think
about policy decisions,

29:28.366 --> 29:33.086 align:middle
and if you don't offer your
reaction function or your way

29:33.086 --> 29:35.226 align:middle
of thinking about it,
how concerned are you

29:35.226 --> 29:37.406 align:middle
that you're ceding
control of the narrative?

29:37.406 --> 29:37.986 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

29:37.986 --> 29:43.196 align:middle
So, not very concerned-that's
a short answer to the question.

29:43.576 --> 29:47.596 align:middle
When some people that
follow the Fed say, "Well,

29:47.596 --> 29:50.416 align:middle
we don't want your forecast-we
don't want your dot-we just want

29:50.416 --> 29:52.666 align:middle
your reaction function," part

29:52.666 --> 29:55.426 align:middle
of me hears the-"What we
really want is your forecast;

29:55.606 --> 29:56.916 align:middle
what we really want
is your dot."

29:57.776 --> 30:02.536 align:middle
In terms of reaction function,
let me sort of disabuse people

30:02.536 --> 30:05.186 align:middle
of a question that may or may
not be real and be out there.

30:06.306 --> 30:10.526 align:middle
Any central banker,
especially a central banker

30:10.526 --> 30:14.806 align:middle
where the labor markets
are more or less

30:15.036 --> 30:18.876 align:middle
at equilibrium-any
central banker, when he

30:18.876 --> 30:24.086 align:middle
or she sees underlying
inflation moving higher-he

30:24.086 --> 30:26.266 align:middle
or she is more inclined
to tighten policy.

30:27.226 --> 30:29.706 align:middle
Again, when you've achieved
the other side of your mandate

30:29.846 --> 30:32.776 align:middle
and you see underlying
inflation falling,

30:33.936 --> 30:36.306 align:middle
he's more inclined
to loosen policy.

30:36.886 --> 30:38.756 align:middle
That's my reaction function,

30:38.826 --> 30:43.926 align:middle
and I don't suspect it will
cause people to not continue

30:43.926 --> 30:47.476 align:middle
to pry for more because the
truth is, for a very long time,

30:47.476 --> 30:51.016 align:middle
in a lot of countries, coming
out of the 2008 crisis,

30:51.576 --> 30:55.706 align:middle
where in crisis mode we were
purposely providing a lot

30:55.706 --> 30:58.276 align:middle
of information-trying to
provide a lot of assurance,

30:58.276 --> 31:00.666 align:middle
trying to tell people exactly
what we're going to do,

31:01.386 --> 31:04.046 align:middle
offering forward
guidance with clarity-as

31:04.046 --> 31:05.916 align:middle
if we're tying our own
hands behind our back.

31:06.466 --> 31:08.636 align:middle
Well, in crisis mode,
that strikes me

31:08.636 --> 31:10.406 align:middle
as a very prudent policy.

31:11.126 --> 31:12.686 align:middle
But in more benign conditions,

31:12.686 --> 31:14.686 align:middle
it strikes me as
worth revisiting.

31:15.306 --> 31:17.426 align:middle
But markets and market
participants

31:17.426 --> 31:21.666 align:middle
and reporters have learned to
devour all that information,

31:21.666 --> 31:24.706 align:middle
so I take seriously
that the pullback

31:24.706 --> 31:27.696 align:middle
of forward guidance
requires some transition.

31:28.086 --> 31:31.726 align:middle
Reform isn't easy, but our
general judgment is going

31:31.726 --> 31:33.536 align:middle
to help us make better
decisions and,

31:33.536 --> 31:35.206 align:middle
in so doing, satisfy our remit.

31:35.786 --> 31:37.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:37.456 --> 31:37.756 align:middle
Paul Wiseman.

31:37.876 --> 31:38.526 align:middle
PAUL WISEMAN.

31:38.526 --> 31:39.076 align:middle
Thank you.

31:39.186 --> 31:39.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:39.536 --> 31:40.916 align:middle
It's coming.

31:40.996 --> 31:44.316 align:middle
There's a mic coming.

31:44.886 --> 31:45.446 align:middle
PAUL WISEMAN.

31:45.716 --> 31:46.006 align:middle
Thank you.

31:46.006 --> 31:49.506 align:middle
When you talk about the 2
percent inflation target,

31:49.776 --> 31:52.766 align:middle
what measure are you relying on?

31:53.346 --> 31:53.806 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

31:53.806 --> 31:55.516 align:middle
Yeah, so I'll give two answers.

31:55.516 --> 31:59.016 align:middle
First, let me give the
proper, standard answer.

31:59.016 --> 32:04.596 align:middle
The, the Federal Reserve every
January outlines a statement

32:04.596 --> 32:08.106 align:middle
of purposes and strategy, and
in that strategy document,

32:08.106 --> 32:10.576 align:middle
which I believe was dated
January of this year,

32:11.146 --> 32:14.626 align:middle
it describes a measure
of PCE inflation

32:14.626 --> 32:16.936 align:middle
as the-as the objective
function there.

32:17.706 --> 32:20.916 align:middle
I have enough of my-so that,

32:20.916 --> 32:22.376 align:middle
that's our number-we're
sticking with it.

32:22.646 --> 32:24.756 align:middle
Who knows, come after
next January,

32:24.756 --> 32:26.406 align:middle
what we might say
about strategy.

32:26.596 --> 32:29.416 align:middle
I suspect the task forces might,
might have something to add.

32:30.156 --> 32:33.836 align:middle
But I'll say this: Some
version of the Lucas critique,

32:34.306 --> 32:38.626 align:middle
some version of Goodhart's law,
in economics should remind us

32:38.956 --> 32:45.326 align:middle
that when we talk about measures
of inflation or something else

32:45.676 --> 32:48.376 align:middle
and we describe those
measures as being consistent

32:48.376 --> 32:51.706 align:middle
with our objectives,
we might make them

32:51.706 --> 32:53.776 align:middle
such that they're not
very good measures

32:53.776 --> 32:55.206 align:middle
or very good objectives.

32:56.046 --> 32:58.846 align:middle
Broadly, if you said to me
standing in front of you,

32:58.846 --> 33:01.526 align:middle
"I abide fully by the
strategy document-we're going

33:01.526 --> 33:06.616 align:middle
to deliver 2 percent inflation,"
and not a whisper more-but

33:06.616 --> 33:09.836 align:middle
to achieve that, I'm
looking at a broader set

33:09.836 --> 33:11.746 align:middle
of inflation data than PCE.

33:12.376 --> 33:15.426 align:middle
So without sort of fully
revealing my cards,

33:15.816 --> 33:18.046 align:middle
I'm trying to understand,

33:18.046 --> 33:22.606 align:middle
like my colleagues-what's the
underlying generalized change

33:22.606 --> 33:24.726 align:middle
in prices that are
happening in the economy?

33:25.106 --> 33:26.826 align:middle
It is not a perfect science.

33:27.386 --> 33:29.716 align:middle
I might have said 42 days ago,

33:29.716 --> 33:31.116 align:middle
"I've got a task
force for that."

33:31.706 --> 33:34.186 align:middle
But we have a data project
that's trying to look

33:34.186 --> 33:36.726 align:middle
and see whether we
can't separate the noise

33:36.726 --> 33:37.586 align:middle
from the signal.

33:38.306 --> 33:40.476 align:middle
And so if you would hear
a message from me-yes,

33:40.476 --> 33:43.076 align:middle
I care about what
the PCE "prints" are.

33:43.076 --> 33:44.876 align:middle
I care about what
the contributions are

33:44.876 --> 33:46.626 align:middle
from CPI and everything else.

33:47.256 --> 33:51.226 align:middle
But my-my lens is
broader than that,

33:51.226 --> 33:53.106 align:middle
even though the remit
is quite narrow.

33:53.106 --> 33:54.286 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:54.696 --> 33:55.176 align:middle
Mike McKee.

33:56.176 --> 33:56.786 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

34:00.716 --> 34:02.816 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

34:02.926 --> 34:07.756 align:middle
I'm struggling a little bit with
some of what you've said today,

34:07.816 --> 34:10.076 align:middle
and maybe you can
help clarify this.

34:11.206 --> 34:15.196 align:middle
You've said over and over
again that your job is to bring

34:15.196 --> 34:18.456 align:middle
down prices-to get prices
stable-to hit your target

34:18.456 --> 34:20.056 align:middle
and that you will
hit your target.

34:20.786 --> 34:23.626 align:middle
The market says you're not there

34:23.626 --> 34:25.986 align:middle
yet because they've
raised rates.

34:26.526 --> 34:30.806 align:middle
But all you've talked about
today is talking about it,

34:31.046 --> 34:32.526 align:middle
and it's not like members

34:32.526 --> 34:35.366 align:middle
of the Committee weren't there
before you talking about it.

34:35.796 --> 34:38.256 align:middle
So I guess what the American
people might be asking is,

34:39.016 --> 34:40.036 align:middle
what are you waiting for?

34:40.516 --> 34:40.856 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

34:41.276 --> 34:43.296 align:middle
Yeah. So, believe it or not,

34:43.296 --> 34:46.476 align:middle
this press conference is
not all I've done today.

34:46.476 --> 34:48.886 align:middle
We have spent an
inordinate amount of time

34:48.886 --> 34:52.916 align:middle
in the last two-two
days-two weeks looking

34:52.916 --> 34:55.306 align:middle
at our monetary policy strategy,

34:55.666 --> 35:01.296 align:middle
evaluating our tools-thinking
hard about the sources of data

35:01.606 --> 35:04.256 align:middle
that we have at our
disposal and we wish we had.

35:04.966 --> 35:07.496 align:middle
We've also thought hard
about the period ahead.

35:07.946 --> 35:12.036 align:middle
What among these questions will
be answered with more clarity?

35:12.036 --> 35:17.116 align:middle
Certainly not certainty-so
the decision we made today-the

35:17.116 --> 35:21.036 align:middle
discussion we had in that
room was the farthest thing

35:21.036 --> 35:22.786 align:middle
from inertia I can imagine.

35:23.406 --> 35:26.116 align:middle
As a point estimate
at this very moment,

35:26.496 --> 35:28.666 align:middle
in a choice between
two alternatives,

35:28.916 --> 35:32.476 align:middle
you heard the results of
it, but I would tell you

35:32.476 --> 35:34.856 align:middle
that this discussion
was far more robust.

35:35.426 --> 35:38.656 align:middle
And our thinking about
how best to achieve

35:38.656 --> 35:40.316 align:middle
that target is advanced,

35:40.726 --> 35:42.626 align:middle
and over the coming
months I expect it

35:42.626 --> 35:44.706 align:middle
to be advanced much
more significantly.

35:45.446 --> 35:48.406 align:middle
If you were to sort of-if I were
to steal a follow-up question,

35:48.406 --> 35:50.076 align:middle
I won't let you-you
won't be giving it up.

35:50.196 --> 35:53.366 align:middle
If I were to steal a
follow-up question, "Well,

35:53.596 --> 35:55.866 align:middle
what's-what's the world think
about what you've done?"

35:56.536 --> 35:59.266 align:middle
I would again reiterate
what we do isn't just

35:59.266 --> 36:02.856 align:middle
about what we say-it's not
just about what we do-we're

36:02.856 --> 36:04.626 align:middle
in the performance business.

36:06.416 --> 36:11.026 align:middle
And so, so if I look
at the Treasury curve,

36:11.286 --> 36:13.896 align:middle
if I look at the dollar, if
I look at a lot of things

36:13.896 --> 36:16.366 align:middle
that are internals inside
of financial markets,

36:17.046 --> 36:19.466 align:middle
I think what they're
broadly saying is

36:19.816 --> 36:24.026 align:middle
that this Committee does
own it-has the credibility

36:24.026 --> 36:24.796 align:middle
to deliver it.

36:25.326 --> 36:28.206 align:middle
And they believe, like
I do, that we will.

36:28.766 --> 36:31.306 align:middle
But I don't want to leave you

36:31.306 --> 36:33.076 align:middle
with a misimpression-we've
got no magic wand.

36:33.076 --> 36:35.936 align:middle
This isn't something that
we're going to be able

36:35.936 --> 36:37.536 align:middle
to carry out in days or weeks.

36:38.196 --> 36:40.566 align:middle
But we're going to deliver
on the responsibility

36:40.566 --> 36:41.846 align:middle
that Congress gave us,

36:42.186 --> 36:44.336 align:middle
and today's meeting-and
the preparation

36:44.336 --> 36:47.226 align:middle
for today's meeting-was
an important step towards

36:47.226 --> 36:48.016 align:middle
that destination.

36:48.506 --> 36:49.146 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

36:49.276 --> 36:52.226 align:middle
I'd like to follow up on
the task forces as well

36:52.596 --> 36:55.126 align:middle
and ask-what vetting
did you do of the people

36:55.126 --> 36:56.856 align:middle
that you appointed
to the task forces?

36:56.856 --> 36:59.856 align:middle
In particular, given Marc
Andreessen's substantial

36:59.856 --> 37:03.626 align:middle
political spending-$25
million in just the past year

37:03.626 --> 37:08.356 align:middle
to back candidates who oppose
stricter I-AI regulation-how can

37:08.356 --> 37:09.556 align:middle
the public be confident

37:09.646 --> 37:12.616 align:middle
that a committee he co-chairs
will provide an independent

37:12.616 --> 37:16.506 align:middle
assessment of AI's economic
effects, rather than one aligned

37:16.506 --> 37:18.086 align:middle
with the interests
of the AI industry?

37:18.086 --> 37:18.396 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

37:18.396 --> 37:24.576 align:middle
Yeah, so I selected
15 incredible subject

37:24.576 --> 37:28.886 align:middle
market-subject matter
experts to tackle five

37:28.886 --> 37:31.426 align:middle
of the most important
questions that,

37:31.426 --> 37:32.706 align:middle
if we get the answers right,

37:33.206 --> 37:35.676 align:middle
we're going to do a far
better job in delivering.

37:35.676 --> 37:37.466 align:middle
And if we get the answers
wrong, we have a problem.

37:38.396 --> 37:41.876 align:middle
The comfort that I can give
you and your listeners is,

37:41.876 --> 37:43.306 align:middle
we're the decisionmakers.

37:43.616 --> 37:48.016 align:middle
The Chairman of the Board of the
Federal Reserve and the members

37:48.016 --> 37:51.626 align:middle
of the Board and the
FOMC-we will be the consumers

37:52.176 --> 37:55.966 align:middle
of the outputs from five
different committees.

37:56.686 --> 38:00.396 align:middle
The judgments we're making
will be informed by-but not

38:00.396 --> 38:03.296 align:middle
at all determined
by-these outside groups.

38:03.736 --> 38:05.296 align:middle
My theory of the case

38:05.296 --> 38:09.186 align:middle
in establishing the task
forces were to pick people

38:09.186 --> 38:15.206 align:middle
with extraordinary talent, depth
of expertise, and a divergence

38:15.206 --> 38:19.306 align:middle
of views inside every
committee so they, too,

38:19.306 --> 38:21.566 align:middle
can have a family fight.

38:21.976 --> 38:25.176 align:middle
This is not outsourcing to
people that aren't known

38:25.176 --> 38:28.766 align:middle
and haven't been vetted-this
is seeing whether new ideas can

38:28.766 --> 38:30.766 align:middle
catalyze a broader, better,

38:30.766 --> 38:33.256 align:middle
more-informed discussion
inside the room.

38:33.886 --> 38:36.016 align:middle
And I'm very confident
that we're going

38:36.016 --> 38:36.716 align:middle
to be able to do that.

38:36.716 --> 38:39.696 align:middle
I am impressed by the
credentials of these 15 people.

38:40.176 --> 38:43.726 align:middle
And, full disclosure, I've
known almost all of them

38:43.726 --> 38:46.476 align:middle
for a very long time,
and I think they're going

38:46.476 --> 38:48.486 align:middle
to give their best
views on the subject.

38:49.006 --> 38:51.306 align:middle
But, ultimately, these are
decisions we're going to make,

38:51.306 --> 38:53.966 align:middle
and we're accountable to
our oversight committees

38:53.966 --> 38:56.506 align:middle
and to the remit Congress
gave us to deliver.

38:57.786 --> 38:58.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:58.296 --> 39:00.766 align:middle
Ann. ANN SAPHIR.

39:02.686 --> 39:04.476 align:middle
Hi, Ann Saphir with Reuters.

39:04.476 --> 39:09.276 align:middle
Nice to see you again-so I
need a little help here, too.

39:09.276 --> 39:12.246 align:middle
You've-you've said repeatedly
you have no tolerance

39:12.586 --> 39:13.736 align:middle
for inflation.

39:14.766 --> 39:18.686 align:middle
And yet we are seeing
above-target inflation

39:18.976 --> 39:23.286 align:middle
repeatedly for five years
and through your term so far.

39:23.286 --> 39:25.136 align:middle
And sure, you have
no magic wand,

39:25.806 --> 39:27.486 align:middle
but you have not taken action.

39:27.486 --> 39:28.766 align:middle
You just gave us a little peek

39:28.766 --> 39:30.646 align:middle
at your reaction
function as well.

39:31.106 --> 39:35.626 align:middle
You said that, if underlying
inflation is rising,

39:35.626 --> 39:40.296 align:middle
that you would tend to think
that you might need to tighten.

39:40.526 --> 39:44.376 align:middle
And, with the exception of the
most recent inflation "print,"

39:44.376 --> 39:45.756 align:middle
that is what we've been seeing.

39:46.396 --> 39:50.186 align:middle
So could you explain what
you mean by "no tolerance

39:50.186 --> 39:53.056 align:middle
for inflation" and what
you plan to do about it?

39:53.056 --> 39:53.516 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

39:53.606 --> 39:58.296 align:middle
Sure. So, Ann, I hear from
you what I hear more broadly

39:58.366 --> 40:01.666 align:middle
from households and
businesses: impatience.

40:02.366 --> 40:03.456 align:middle
"Deliver it already."

40:03.656 --> 40:07.316 align:middle
This is not a-this
is not an excuse.

40:07.356 --> 40:11.216 align:middle
This is a fact-this FOMC-this
Board has been in business

40:11.216 --> 40:14.766 align:middle
for eight and a half weeks.

40:14.766 --> 40:19.126 align:middle
This-the, the patience-the
impatience that households

40:19.126 --> 40:23.616 align:middle
and businesses feel have
been going on for 63 months.

40:24.026 --> 40:27.216 align:middle
We are on the job;
we will deliver.

40:27.776 --> 40:31.506 align:middle
We are focused like a laser
on making sure we can do it.

40:32.306 --> 40:34.826 align:middle
But the suggestion that we're
going to be able to do it

40:34.826 --> 40:36.696 align:middle
with our magic wand
is one I want

40:36.696 --> 40:38.486 align:middle
to disabuse you and
everyone else of.

40:39.096 --> 40:42.386 align:middle
But the discussion in the
last two days give me more

40:42.386 --> 40:44.796 align:middle
confidence, even than I had
eight and a half weeks ago.

40:45.326 --> 40:49.606 align:middle
This team that we have at the
FOMC, the support that we have

40:49.686 --> 40:53.446 align:middle
from Board staff, and the new
hard questions we're asking-we

40:53.446 --> 40:54.716 align:middle
need to resolve those and,

40:54.716 --> 40:57.656 align:middle
as we resolve those
questions, get smarter on those.

40:57.656 --> 40:59.066 align:middle
We're going to deliver
on the remit.

40:59.486 --> 41:02.006 align:middle
You don't have to
take my word for it.

41:02.156 --> 41:05.426 align:middle
If you look broadly
at market prices,

41:05.846 --> 41:09.156 align:middle
they are certainly not
saying "all clear,"

41:09.696 --> 41:14.516 align:middle
but they are working in concert
to, to keep us on our toes,

41:14.886 --> 41:17.066 align:middle
and they have tightened
financial conditions

41:17.066 --> 41:18.946 align:middle
in this intermeeting period.

41:19.416 --> 41:25.416 align:middle
And, and that has given us
a-that has provided us some-some

41:25.446 --> 41:29.096 align:middle
comfort that, that
we've got the ability

41:29.096 --> 41:30.466 align:middle
and capability to deliver.

41:31.116 --> 41:31.426 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

41:31.426 --> 41:35.786 align:middle
And, and is your read,
or your, I guess,

41:35.786 --> 41:41.456 align:middle
faith in markets' ability
to sort of make a judgment

41:41.456 --> 41:47.726 align:middle
and then you to take signal from
that judgment-is that-how does

41:48.446 --> 41:52.426 align:middle
that affect you when you come
up on the September meeting

41:52.426 --> 41:57.146 align:middle
and markets are seeing a
near 100 percent chance

41:57.146 --> 41:59.056 align:middle
of a rate hike, as they see now?

42:00.166 --> 42:04.566 align:middle
How does that feed into
your decisionmaking

42:04.566 --> 42:05.176 align:middle
and your thinking?

42:05.176 --> 42:05.496 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

42:05.496 --> 42:09.966 align:middle
So we're not going to be
constrained by market prices.

42:09.966 --> 42:12.716 align:middle
We're not going to be
constrained or take verbatim

42:12.716 --> 42:13.946 align:middle
from what the market's doing.

42:14.546 --> 42:17.616 align:middle
But I think it's useful,
Ann, to understand

42:17.826 --> 42:19.906 align:middle
that markets can be
a very good source

42:19.906 --> 42:23.406 align:middle
of information-not a
determinative source-not a

42:23.406 --> 42:24.336 align:middle
perfect source.

42:24.996 --> 42:28.276 align:middle
But if we're trying
to land the plane

42:28.276 --> 42:34.546 align:middle
and deliver 2 percent inflation
and we take a very useful source

42:34.546 --> 42:36.666 align:middle
of information and
we get it all fogged

42:36.666 --> 42:40.516 align:middle
up by giving it our own
forecast-by providing rolling

42:40.516 --> 42:43.056 align:middle
commentary-I can assure
you that we're going

42:43.056 --> 42:45.436 align:middle
to have less information-less
ability

42:45.436 --> 42:48.536 align:middle
to land the plane successfully
and deliver price stability.

42:48.976 --> 42:50.596 align:middle
We're just trying to make
sure that that source

42:50.596 --> 42:53.686 align:middle
of information is as direct
and unfiltered as possible.

42:54.156 --> 42:57.286 align:middle
It isn't to the exclusion
of data sources and opinions

42:57.286 --> 43:00.626 align:middle
and other surveys, but if
you're hearing from me,

43:00.626 --> 43:02.836 align:middle
we wanted to make sure we're
getting a better source

43:02.836 --> 43:03.666 align:middle
of information.

43:04.126 --> 43:05.766 align:middle
I think, in a relatively
short time, we are.

43:06.616 --> 43:07.656 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:07.926 --> 43:09.636 align:middle
We'll go to Brian Cheung
for the last question.

43:10.326 --> 43:11.376 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

43:11.376 --> 43:12.066 align:middle
Hi there, Chairman Warsh.

43:12.196 --> 43:13.836 align:middle
Brian Cheung with NBC News.

43:14.226 --> 43:17.316 align:middle
So you said you'd be open
to having press conferences

43:17.316 --> 43:18.526 align:middle
when there's news to make.

43:18.526 --> 43:20.726 align:middle
So, today, no change to rates,

43:20.726 --> 43:23.486 align:middle
no forward guidance-for the
average household, I guess,

43:23.976 --> 43:25.886 align:middle
what was the news today?

43:26.916 --> 43:27.346 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

43:27.726 --> 43:31.886 align:middle
So, apparently, it was news
that I had a press conference.

43:31.916 --> 43:34.956 align:middle
Let me just see if I can
offer some clarity on that.

43:35.396 --> 43:39.716 align:middle
Between now and year-end,
my predecessors

43:39.716 --> 43:41.206 align:middle
and the Federal Reserve
committed

43:41.206 --> 43:42.576 align:middle
to press conferences this year.

43:42.926 --> 43:44.836 align:middle
I'm committing to press
conferences this year.

43:45.216 --> 43:46.846 align:middle
That might be news to
the people in this room,

43:46.846 --> 43:50.076 align:middle
and of no particular
interest to your viewers

43:50.076 --> 43:53.126 align:middle
and your-[laughter]-and
your readers back at home.

43:53.596 --> 43:58.546 align:middle
What I can offer as
assurance is that the Fed's

43:58.546 --> 44:02.946 align:middle
on the case-that this
Fed Chairman feels better

44:02.946 --> 44:06.686 align:middle
about this Board and this
Committee's ability to deliver

44:07.006 --> 44:08.876 align:middle
than I did when I showed
up here on the first day.

44:08.876 --> 44:10.226 align:middle
And I showed up pretty
confident.

44:10.936 --> 44:13.766 align:middle
I've been heartened by the
reception that I received.

44:14.356 --> 44:18.226 align:middle
No doubt in some of your
commentaries today you'll talk

44:18.226 --> 44:20.166 align:middle
about a divided Federal Reserve,

44:20.806 --> 44:23.236 align:middle
but that's not the feeling I
felt the last couple of days

44:23.236 --> 44:24.436 align:middle
and the couple days before it.

44:24.656 --> 44:26.996 align:middle
What I felt was a group

44:26.996 --> 44:29.756 align:middle
of professionals-all the
different perspectives,

44:29.756 --> 44:32.966 align:middle
different views, different
judgments-but eager to roll

44:32.966 --> 44:35.956 align:middle
up their sleeves and have
a family fight and eager

44:35.956 --> 44:41.026 align:middle
to reform the way in which
the Fed does policy-a keenness

44:41.026 --> 44:43.736 align:middle
and open-mindedness and
curiosity about that.

44:44.296 --> 44:48.686 align:middle
So we have a far better
chance to deliver on the remit

44:48.686 --> 44:51.576 align:middle
that Congress gave us,
and so I want to leave you

44:51.576 --> 44:55.416 align:middle
with the optimism of
a new central banker

44:56.046 --> 44:58.346 align:middle
that we're committed
as ever to deliver

44:58.466 --> 45:00.416 align:middle
and to offer an assurance
we will.

45:00.936 --> 45:06.106 align:middle
Thank you all very much.

